at.\n\n\n", 'output': 'The current board state shows Black (X) with stones at H8 and H9, forming a vertical "Open 2". White (O) has a stone at I9.\n\n**Evaluation:**\nThe most pressing threat on the board is Black\'s vertical line at H8-H9. If Black is allowed to play at H7 or H10, they will form a "Live 3" (three stones in a row with open ends), which is a very strong attacking position that forces the opponent to defend, handing over the initiative.\n\n**Reason:**\nWhite must block Black\'s vertical development.\n1.  **Defensive:** Playing at **H10** directly blocks the vertical line H8-H9 at the bottom. This prevents Black from extending to a Live 3. If Black were to extend to H7 after White\'s H10, the line would be blocked at one end (H10), making it a "Dead 3" rather than a dangerous Live 3.\n2.  **Positional:** H10 is an excellent positional move because it is adjacent to the White stone at I9. Specifically, I9 and H10 form a diagonal connection. This cooperation between stones allows White to build their own shape and potential threat lines in the future, rather than just passively blocking.\n\nComparing to other moves:\n*   Playing at H7 blocks the top but leaves a gap between H7 and I9, offering less connectivity.\n*   Playing at I8 would create a vertical 2 for White (I8-I9), but it would allow Black to play H10 (forming a Live 3) and seize the initiative.\n\nTherefore, H10 is the most balanced and effective move.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 10}'}
