ts Black from connecting J7 with the existing stone at J8 (occupying J7 separates Black\'s potential cluster). Furthermore, playing J7 places a White stone closer to White\'s existing group (I8, I9, K9) and sets up a potential diagonal line with H5 (H5-I6-J7).\n\nReason: White is forced to defend against Black\'s 3-in-a-row on Row 7. Blocking at **J7** is superior to F7 because it not only neutralizes the immediate threat but also denies Black a strong vertical connection between J7 and J8, while keeping White\'s stones more connected for future counter-play.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 7}'}
