ree\' (F6-G6-H6), which is a very strong forcing move that typically leads to victory if not immediately countered. Furthermore, a Black stone at G6 would create additional diagonal connections (e.g., F5-G6-H7). White has no immediate winning threats (no open 3s or 4s), so the priority is to block Black\'s most dangerous potential move.\n\nReason: By playing at G6, White prevents Black from forming a lethal \'Open Three\' on Row 6. This move also occupies a vital shape point that disrupts Black\'s diagonal connections and connects to White\'s existing stone at H5. While Black still has threats on Row 7 and the diagonal, these are \'Sleeping\' threats that can be managed in subsequent turns. Blocking the \'Open Three\' potential at G6 is the most urgent defensive action to stay in the game and maximize chances of winning later.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "G", "row": 6}'}
